restart for the refused update and the next two letters at the caret;
the recorder shows a composition open at the update in 19 of the 35 runs
of that batch and none in 16. The same update sent again and again was
refused until six refusals stood for one version of the model, then
passed once with its range cut: its text was typed at the end of the
buffer, the lines after it were whole, and nothing read undefined, in 5
runs of 5. An empty text over a range beyond the buffer, sent seven
times with the caret inside the word, changed nothing in 6 runs of 6,
and in the 3 of them where an Undo followed it took back the typed line,
so the passed update had left no undo step.

On an API 33 emulator with Gboard 12.4 and WebView 134, the refusal held
in 15 runs of 15 in the three states, the composition open and untyped
at the update in all five runs of that state. The bound held in 7 of 7:
six refusals, the seventh update passed with its range cut at the end of
the buffer, the eighth refused again. Past the bound an empty text
wholly beyond the buffer changed nothing and one Undo took back the
letter typed before it in 3 of 3, and an empty text from two units after
the caret to five past the buffer deleted to the end of the buffer and
nothing after it in 3 of 3.

With the patch as it is here, the turn and the letter were run 28 times
more on the API 31 emulator, with no load made on the host: 14 of 14 on
a new line came out right, with two more letters typed after the first,
and 12 of 14 on a line reached by a tap. In the two that were not, the
keyboard wrote the letter alone over the whole reopened word, which is
refused, and the letter was lost, as said above. No update of the 28
runs ended beyond the buffer, and the patch without the refusal of such
a range decides every one of their 485 updates the same. On the API 33
emulator with Gboard 12.4 the turn and a letter were right in 10 runs of
10 with it, the keyboard writing the word and the letter together each
time.

Other keyboards, on the nine rules: on the API 33 emulator with Gboard
12.4, which composes English words, twenty runs of the turn and a letter
were right, and fifteen runs of the rules' honest neighbours were right
with no refusal (letters typed and deleted at a word's end, a word taken
from the strip, a letter typed inside a word); in five more a word typed
for correction stayed as typed, because the keyboard offered no
correction in the editor; on an API 36 emulator with Gboard 18.5, Korean
and Japanese lines typed, converted and turned came out as typed in 224
updates with no refusal.

